## Corrected solution

```java
// Returns the length of the longest run of consecutive identical elements.
public int findMaxConsecutive(int[] arr) {
    // Edge case: no elements means there is no run to measure.
    if (arr == null || arr.length == 0) {
        return 0;
    }

    int maxCount     = 1;  // best (longest) run seen so far; at least the 1st element
    int currentCount = 1;  // length of the run that ENDS at index i

    // Walk from the 2nd element and compare each with its predecessor.
    // This way we never read past the end of the array.
    for (int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] == arr[i - 1]) {
            currentCount++;        // same value continues the current run
        } else {
            currentCount = 1;      // a new run starts at this index
        }
        if (currentCount > maxCount) {
            maxCount = currentCount; // record the longest run found so far
        }
    }

    return maxCount;
}
```

### Why this is correct
- **No out‑of‑bounds access:** the largest index read is `arr[i]` with `i = arr.length - 1`, which is valid.
- **Single‑element array:** the loop is skipped, `maxCount` is already `1` → returns `1`. ✔
- **Empty / `null` array:** explicitly returns `0`. ✔
- **Invariant:** after each iteration, `currentCount` equals the length of the consecutive run ending at index `i`, and `maxCount` is the maximum over all such runs → exactly the answer required.

### Walkthrough on `[1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4]`
| i | arr[i] vs arr[i‑1] | currentCount | maxCount |
|---|--------------------|--------------|----------|
| 1 | 2 vs 1 (≠)         | 1            | 1        |
| 2 | 2 vs 2 (=)         | 2            | 2        |
| 3 | 2 vs 2 (=)         | 3            | 3        |
| 4 | 3 vs 2 (≠)         | 1            | 3        |
| 5 | 3 vs 3 (=)         | 2            | 3        |
| 6 | 4 vs 3 (≠)         | 1            | 3        |

Returns **3**, matching the expected result (the three `2`'s).

