# Correct approach

A correct solution should do the following:

1. Count the number of mandatory queens.
2. Check that no two mandatory queens attack each other.
3. Precompute attack information from mandatory queens using bit masks.
4. Recursively process rows from `0` to `7`.
5. If a row already has a mandatory queen, skip it without placing an additional queen.
6. If a row has no mandatory queen, place exactly one additional queen in that row.
7. Only place additional queens on `0` cells.
8. Use bit masks to check column and diagonal attacks in constant time.
9. Do not modify the original board, or restore it exactly if mutation is used.

Because the board is `8x8` and we need exactly 8 non-attacking queens, every row must contain exactly one queen. This lets us use a row-by-row backtracking search.

---

# Correct Java implementation

Here is a complete corrected implementation:

```java
public class QueenPlacements {
    private static final int N = 8;

    public int countValidQueenPlacements(int[][] board) {
        int mandatoryCount = 0;

        // Bit masks for currently occupied columns and diagonals.
        // These are initialized with the fixed mandatory queens.
        int colMask = 0;

        // Main diagonal index: r - c + N - 1
        // This ranges from 0 to 14 for an 8x8 board.
        int diag1Mask = 0;

        // Anti-diagonal index: r + c
        // This also ranges from 0 to 14 for an 8x8 board.
        int diag2Mask = 0;

        // rowHasMandatory[r] is true if row r already has a fixed queen.
        boolean[] rowHasMandatory = new boolean[N];

        // Process all mandatory queens.
        for (int r = 0; r < N; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < N; c++) {
                if (board[r][c] == 1) {
                    mandatoryCount++;

                    // Two mandatory queens in the same row is impossible.
                    if (rowHasMandatory[r]) {
                        return 0;
                    }

                    int colBit = 1 << c;
                    int d1Bit = 1 << (r - c + N - 1);
                    int d2Bit = 1 << (r + c);

                    // If two mandatory queens share a column or diagonal,
                    // no valid solution exists.
                    if ((colMask & colBit) != 0 ||
                        (diag1Mask & d1Bit) != 0 ||
                        (diag2Mask & d2Bit) != 0) {
                        return 0;
                    }

                    colMask |= colBit;
                    diag1Mask |= d1Bit;
                    diag2Mask |= d2Bit;

                    rowHasMandatory[r] = true;
                }
            }
        }

        // More than 8 mandatory queens is impossible.
        if (mandatoryCount > N) {
            return 0;
        }

        // nonMandatorySuffix[r] = number of rows from r through 7
        // that do NOT contain a mandatory queen.
        //
        // In any valid final board, each of those rows must receive
        // exactly one additional queen.
        int[] nonMandatorySuffix = new int[N + 1];
        for (int r = N - 1; r >= 0; r--) {
            nonMandatorySuffix[r] =
                nonMandatorySuffix[r + 1] + (rowHasMandatory[r] ? 0 : 1);
        }

        int queensToPlace = N - mandatoryCount;

        return backtrack(
            0,
            queensToPlace,
            colMask,
            diag1Mask,
            diag2Mask,
            board,
            rowHasMandatory,
            nonMandatorySuffix
        );
    }

    private int backtrack(
        int row,
        int queensLeft,
        int colMask,
        int diag1Mask,
        int diag2Mask,
        int[][] board,
        boolean[] rowHasMandatory,
        int[] nonMandatorySuffix
    ) {
        // Finished all rows.
        if (row == N) {
            return queensLeft == 0 ? 1 : 0;
        }

        // Prune if the number of queens we still need to place
        // does not match the number of remaining rows that need one.
        if (queensLeft != nonMandatorySuffix[row]) {
            return 0;
        }

        // If this row already has a mandatory queen,
        // we cannot place another queen in this row.
        if (rowHasMandatory[row]) {
            return backtrack(
                row + 1,
                queensLeft,
                colMask,
                diag1Mask,
                diag2Mask,
                board,
                rowHasMandatory,
                nonMandatorySuffix
            );
        }

        int count = 0;

        // This row has no mandatory queen, so we must place
        // exactly one additional queen in this row.
        for (int col = 0; col < N; col++) {
            // Only empty available squares can receive a new queen.
            if (board[row][col] != 0) {
                continue;
            }

            int colBit = 1 << col;
            int d1Bit = 1 << (row - col + N - 1);
            int d2Bit = 1 << (row + col);

            // Check column and diagonal attacks using bit masks.
            if ((colMask & colBit) == 0 &&
                (diag1Mask & d1Bit) == 0 &&
                (diag2Mask & d2Bit) == 0) {

                // Place this additional queen conceptually.
                // We do not modify the input board.
                count += backtrack(
                    row + 1,
                    queensLeft - 1,
                    colMask | colBit,
                    diag1Mask | d1Bit,
                    diag2Mask | d2Bit,
                    board,
                    rowHasMandatory,
                    nonMandatorySuffix
                );
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```

